  "query_preview": "..Scattered showers have been ongoing this morning\nand afternoon as moisture begins to stream in as part of an\natmospheric river ahead of a Pacific low. Rainfall amounts have\nbeen relatively light, generally 0.01\"-0.4\" with no lightning. \nInstability is fairly limited, but can't rule out a lightning \nstrike or two this afternoon while the sun is up. Hi-res \nguidance has struggled with this shower activity, but expect it \nto continue on and off through the afternoon.\n\nPrecipitable water will continue to increase this evening and\novernight ranging from 1.6\" in western Pima County to 1.25\"\nalong the AZ/NM border by Sunday morning. The Pacific low will\ndig south this evening and overnight, then move inland Sunday.\nThis will lead to widespread moderate to heavy showers with embedded\nthunderstorms tonight into Monday. \n\nOver the past couple model cycles hi-res guidance shows a first\nwave of moderate showers this evening and part of the overnight\nhours from Tucson westward. Shower activity is expected to \nincrease west of Tucson between 4-6 pm then increase in coverage\nthrough the evening. General rainfall totals through midnight \nlook to be 0.25\"-0.75\". Sunday morning showers will likely still\nmainly be from Tucson west then increase in coverage to the \neast late morning into the afternoon as the core of the trough \nmoves through Arizona. A strong jet associated with the low will\naid shower and thunderstorm development. Heavy rainfall leading\nto flash flooding and wash/river flooding will be the main \nthreats. However the jet will also help create an environment \nconducive to strong to maybe even severe wind gusts in stronger \nthunderstorms. SPC has southeast Arizona in a marginal risk for \nsevere weather Sunday. However the main limiting factor in this \nwill be instability which may be lacking, at least in Arizona. \nBetter instability will be in place over New Mexico. Regardless \nconvection within the widespread showers will likely be \nresponsible for the heaviest rainfall rates. Activity is \nexpected to decrease from west to east Sunday night into Monday.\nA Flood Watch is in effect from 11 PM this evening through 5 AM\nMonday. QPF hasn't fluctuated by much over the past 12-24 hours\nwith Pinal County, northern Graham and Greenlee Counties, and \nthe Sky Islands north of I-10 expected to see the heaviest rain.\nThe REFS shows the Catalinas, Pinalenos, and White Mountains \nhaving a 20-60 percent chance to see 3+ inches through Monday \nmorning. Below is a look at the expected totals and high/low end\ntotals.\n\n\n-Expected: Valleys 0.50\"-1.75\", Mountains 1\"-3+\"   \n\n-Low end (9 in 10 chance):\n Valleys 0.25\"-1.25\", Mountains 0.50\"-1.5\" \n  \n-High end (1 in 10 chance): \n Valleys 1.75\"-3.25\", Mountains 2\"-4\" locally higher\n\nWith the expected rain on Sunday into early Monday we will see \na response in area rivers, creeks, and washes across SE AZ. See\nhydrology section below for further details.\n\nMonday morning will see lingering showers, but no significant\nrainfall is expected. In the wake of the trough, brief ridging\nwill be in place through Tuesday. Then a couple shortwaves moves\nthrough the region during the latter half of the work week. The\natmosphere dries out fairly quickly Monday leading to dry\nconditions for the work week. High temperatures will be 4-7\ndegrees below normal through the work week.",
